					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/epif" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1355808); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1355808&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608498501" alt="Epif" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1355808)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/epif" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1355808); return false;" >Epif</a></span> <span class="odate time_1336197656 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">05 May 2012 06:00</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1438615">
									

<p>This story is centered around getting confused over simple scientific facts, such as getting prefixes mixed up, so I thought I should say something about the &quot;0.5K&quot; temperature mentioned at the beginning of the story: <em>Outerspace is 3K. The scientist is acting like that was no big achievement.</em> I mean, it's not a crucial bit to the plot of this short story, but it shatters the suspension of disbelief when a scientist acts like that's nothing.</p>
<p>Also, if this wasn't linked from <a href="/technical-words">Technical Words</a>, then I wouldn't have said anything, but it is, so I have.</p>
<p>Other than that, though, it's a good story.</p>

							</div>

								
					
